\title{Rearranging the Primes: Density and Rigidity of\\ Displacement Ratios in the Prime Hotel}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study rearrangements of an infinite indexed family of guests, where room $n$ of an infinite hotel is occupied by the $n$-th prime $p_n$. A rearrangement is a permutation $\sigma$ of the natural numbers; after rearranging, room $n$ holds the prime $p_{\sigma(n)}$. We measure the disruption caused by $\sigma$ through the \emph{displacement ratio} $R_\sigma(n) = p_{\sigma(n)}/p_n$, and we call $\sigma$ \emph{well behaved} when $R_\sigma(n) \to 1$, i.e.\ when guests asymptotically retain their magnitude. We prove three results. First, every finitely supported permutation is well behaved, with displacement ratio eventually equal to $1$. Second---the central result---the well-behaved permutations are \emph{dense} in the symmetric group $\Sym(\N)$ under the topology of pointwise convergence: every permutation is matched on any finite initial segment by a well-behaved one. Third, the property is not universal: we construct an explicit involution, assembled from a sparse family of prime-doubling long-range swaps, whose displacement ratio is $\ge 2$ infinitely often and hence fails to converge. All three results depend only on the primes being a strictly increasing unbounded sequence, so they hold verbatim for any such sequence; the Prime Number Theorem is not required. We conclude with conjectures, in particular a proposed characterization of well-behaved permutations via $\sigma(n)/n \to 1$, and a discussion of how to quantify the ``density'' of well-behaved permutations in the sense of Baire category.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Hilbert's hotel is a classical illustration of the counterintuitive arithmetic of infinite sets: a fully occupied hotel with rooms indexed by $\N$ can still accommodate new guests by relabeling. We adopt the hotel as a staging ground for a question about the \emph{stability of the prime numbers under rearrangement}.

Assign to room $n \in \N$ the $n$-th prime $p_n$. Because there are infinitely many primes, every room is occupied. A \emph{rearrangement of the guests} is a bijection $\sigma : \N \to \N$; we interpret it as sending the guest originally in room $\sigma(n)$ into room $n$, so that after the rearrangement room $n$ holds the prime $p_{\sigma(n)}$.

The quantity of interest is how much the magnitude of a room's occupant changes. We define the \textbf{displacement ratio}
\[
R_\sigma(n) = \frac{p_{\sigma(n)}}{p_n},
\]
and declare $\sigma$ \textbf{well behaved} when $R_\sigma(n) \to 1$ as $n \to \infty$: asymptotically, the prime now in room $n$ is essentially the same size as the one that used to be there.

Two questions drive the paper: How plentiful are well-behaved rearrangements? (They are \emph{dense}.) Is well-behavedness automatic? (It is not.) A pleasant feature of the analysis is its economy: the only property of $n \mapsto p_n$ used is that it is strictly increasing and tends to infinity, so all main results hold for an arbitrary strictly increasing unbounded sequence, and the Prime Number Theorem is not invoked.

\section{Definitions and basic properties of the prime rooms}

Let $p : \N \to \N$ enumerate the primes in increasing order, $p_0 = 2, p_1 = 3, p_2 = 5, \dots$ (indexing from $0$).

\begin{proposition}[Basic properties]\label{prop:basic}
(1) $p_n$ is prime for every $n$; in particular $p_n > 0$ and $(p_n : \R) \ne 0$.
(2) $p$ is strictly monotone: $m < n \implies p_m < p_n$; hence $p$ is injective.
(3) $p_n \to \infty$ as $n \to \infty$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Enumerating an infinite set of naturals in increasing order yields a strictly monotone function whose values are exactly that set; the set of primes is infinite (Euclid), giving (1)--(2). A strictly monotone function $\N \to \N$ tends to infinity, giving (3).
\end{proof}

Everything below uses only Proposition~\ref{prop:basic}(2)--(3).

\begin{definition}[Displacement ratio]
For $\sigma \in \Sym(\N)$ and $n \in \N$, set $R_\sigma(n) = p_{\sigma(n)}/p_n \in \R$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Well behaved]
A permutation $\sigma \in \Sym(\N)$ is \emph{well behaved} if $R_\sigma(n) \to 1$ as $n \to \infty$.
\end{definition}

We give $\Sym(\N) \subseteq \N^{\N}$ the topology of \textbf{pointwise convergence}: a basic neighborhood of $\sigma$ is $U_{\sigma, N} = \{ \tau : \tau(i) = \sigma(i) \text{ for all } i < N \}$, $N \in \N$. A set $S \subseteq \Sym(\N)$ is dense iff every $U_{\sigma, N}$ meets $S$.

\section{Finitely supported rearrangements are well behaved}

The \emph{support} of $\sigma$ is $\supp(\sigma) = \{ n : \sigma(n) \ne n \}$; $\sigma$ is \emph{finitely supported} if $\supp(\sigma)$ is finite.

\begin{lemma}[Eventually identity]\label{lem:evid}
If $\supp(\sigma)$ is finite, there is $N$ with $\sigma(n) = n$ for all $n \ge N$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A finite set of naturals is bounded above by some $M$; take $N = M + 1$. Any $n \ge N$ lies outside $\supp(\sigma)$, so $\sigma(n) = n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Eventual identity implies well behaved]\label{lem:evwb}
If there is $N$ with $\sigma(n) = n$ for all $n \ge N$, then $\sigma$ is well behaved; indeed $R_\sigma(n) = 1$ for all $n \ge N$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $n \ge N$, $\sigma(n) = n$, so $R_\sigma(n) = p_n/p_n = 1$ (using $p_n \ne 0$). A sequence eventually equal to $1$ converges to $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite support $\Rightarrow$ well behaved]\label{thm:fin}
Every finitely supported permutation is well behaved.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Lemmas~\ref{lem:evid} and~\ref{lem:evwb}.
\end{proof}

In particular the identity permutation is well behaved.

\section{Density of well-behaved rearrangements}

\begin{lemma}[Finite-support approximation]\label{lem:approx}
For every $\sigma \in \Sym(\N)$ and every $N \in \N$, there is a finitely supported permutation $\tau$ with $\tau(i) = \sigma(i)$ for all $i < N$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $N$. For $N = 0$ take $\tau = \mathrm{id}$; the condition is vacuous and $\mathrm{id}$ has empty support.

For the step, suppose $\tau$ is finitely supported with $\tau(i) = \sigma(i)$ for all $i < N$. Set
\[
\tau' = \swap(\tau(N), \sigma(N)) \circ \tau,
\]
the transposition being the identity if $\tau(N) = \sigma(N)$. Then $\tau'(N) = \swap(\tau(N), \sigma(N))(\tau(N)) = \sigma(N)$. For $i < N$ we have $\tau(i) = \sigma(i)$; since $\tau$ is injective and $i \ne N$, $\tau(i) \ne \tau(N)$, and $\tau(i) = \sigma(i) = \sigma(N)$ would force $i = N$ by injectivity of $\sigma$, so $\tau(i) \ne \sigma(N)$. Hence the swap fixes $\tau(i)$ and $\tau'(i) = \sigma(i)$. Finally $\supp(\tau') \subseteq \supp(\tau) \cup \{\tau(N), \sigma(N)\}$ is finite.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Density theorem]\label{thm:dense}
For every $\sigma \in \Sym(\N)$ and every $N \in \N$, there is a well-behaved permutation $\tau$ with $\tau(i) = \sigma(i)$ for all $i < N$. Equivalently, the well-behaved permutations are dense in $\Sym(\N)$ for the topology of pointwise convergence.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:approx} gives a finitely supported $\tau$ agreeing with $\sigma$ on $\{0, \dots, N-1\}$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:fin} it is well behaved. Thus $U_{\sigma, N}$ meets the well-behaved set, and since these neighborhoods form a basis, density follows.
\end{proof}

\section{Well-behavedness is not universal}

\begin{lemma}[Prime doubling]\label{lem:double}
For every $m \in \N$ there is $b > m$ with $2 p_m \le p_b$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Since $p_n \to \infty$, eventually $p_n > 2 p_m$; choose such an $n$ also exceeding $m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Jump sequence]
Define $j : \N \to \N$ by $j_0 = 0$ and $j_{k+1} =$ a witness $b > j_k$ with $2 p_{j_k} \le p_b$ from Lemma~\ref{lem:double}.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Properties of the jump sequence]\label{lem:jump}
(1) $j_k < j_{k+1}$ for all $k$; hence $j$ is strictly monotone and injective, and $k \le j_k$. (2) $2 p_{j_k} \le p_{j_{k+1}}$ for all $k$.
\end{lemma}

Define the \emph{toggle} $t(k) = k+1$ if $k$ is even and $t(k) = k-1$ if $k$ is odd; it is an involution with $t(2j) = 2j+1$.

\begin{definition}[Bad rearrangement]
Define $\beta : \N \to \N$ by $\beta(n) = j_{t(k)}$ if $n = j_k$ for some (unique) $k$, and $\beta(n) = n$ otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[$\beta$ is an involution]\label{lem:inv}
$\beta(\beta(n)) = n$ for all $n$; hence $\beta \in \Sym(\N)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $n$ is not a landmark, $\beta(n) = n$. If $n = j_k$, then $\beta(n) = j_{t(k)}$, a landmark, so $\beta(j_{t(k)}) = j_{t(t(k))} = j_k = n$, using that $t$ is an involution. A self-inverse map $\N \to \N$ is a bijection.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Not universal]\label{thm:bad}
The permutation $\beta$ is not well behaved. Precisely, $R_\beta(n) \ge 2$ at every even landmark $n = j_{2i}$, hence for infinitely many $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $n = j_{2i}$, $t(2i) = 2i+1$, so $\beta(n) = j_{2i+1}$ and
\[
R_\beta(n) = \frac{p_{j_{2i+1}}}{p_{j_{2i}}} \ge \frac{2 p_{j_{2i}}}{p_{j_{2i}}} = 2
\]
by Lemma~\ref{lem:jump}(2). The $j_{2i}$ are distinct and unbounded, so this occurs infinitely often; a sequence $\ge 2$ infinitely often cannot converge to $1$.
\end{proof}

\section{Generality and discussion}

The proofs use only monotonicity (for Lemma~\ref{lem:approx} and injectivity of $j$) and unboundedness (for Lemma~\ref{lem:double}). Hence, for \emph{any} strictly increasing $a : \N \to \N$ with $a_n \to \infty$: finitely supported permutations are well behaved, the well-behaved permutations are dense, and some permutation is not well behaved. The dichotomy is a feature of infinite monotone growth, not of arithmetic.

The primes re-enter for sharper questions. We conjecture that $\sigma$ is well behaved iff $p_{\sigma(n)}/p_n \to 1$, equivalently (via $p_n \sim n \log n$) iff $\sigma(n)/n \to 1$, making well-behavedness an asymptotic unit-distortion invariant. The swap-even/odd permutation $n \mapsto n \oplus 1$ should be well behaved by $p_{n+1}/p_n \to 1$ (prime gaps $o(p_n)$). Topologically the well-behaved set is dense but meager; quantifying its ``density'' via Baire category or a natural measure on $\Sym(\N)$ is open. One may also study permutations whose displacement ratio has a limit $L \ne 1$, and replace primes by other monotone unbounded sequences.

\section{Conclusion}

The prime hotel exhibits a crisp trichotomy: finite reshuffles are trivially well behaved; well-behaved reshuffles are dense---indistinguishable from arbitrary ones on any finite window; yet well-behavedness is not automatic, as an explicit involution built from prime-doubling long-range swaps distorts magnitudes by a factor $\ge 2$ infinitely often. The primes are robust under rearrangement, but only almost; that qualification is the mathematics.

